by default, so a dead pod is only

230
00:16:45,010 --> 00:16:47,683
noticed when the whole step timed out.

231
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:51,816
Restate suspend the quiet
step after one minute.

232
00:16:52,344 --> 00:16:55,519
Hatchet times out after sixty seconds.

233
00:16:56,251 --> 00:16:58,273
Even Claude Code has one.

234
00:16:58,701 --> 00:17:04,069
When you run it from a script with
nobody watching, it kills any helper

235
00:17:04,075 --> 00:17:06,348
agent that stays quiet for ten minutes.

236
00:17:06,996 --> 00:17:12,816
In one run, it killed nine of
its eleven helpers, and the

237
00:17:12,816 --> 00:17:14,525
job still reported success.

238
00:17:15,070 --> 00:17:17,904
And I hit this in my own pipeline.

239
00:17:17,918 --> 00:17:22,354
My CI fix step started with
a twenty-minute timeout.

240
00:17:22,634 --> 00:17:30,508
Real fixes kept running longer, so I kept
raising it, and it ended up at four hours.

241
00:17:30,724 --> 00:17:35,322
So check the defaults before
you check the features.

242
00:17:36,396 --> 00:17:40,689
And here, my hard constraint did its job.

243
00:17:40,778 --> 00:17:47,056
By default, all these hosted coding
agents run in the vendor sandbox.

244
00:17:47,667 --> 00:17:50,749
Claude Code on the web
runs on Anthropic machine.

245
00:17:51,762 --> 00:17:57,444
It now has self-hosted runners that work
inside your network, but they are in beta

246
00:17:57,590 --> 00:18:00,734
and only on team and enterprise plan.

247
00:18:01,225 --> 00:18:10,788
Codex Cloud runs on OpenAI's, so
OpenAI's machines and its VPN is meant

248
00:18:10,792 --> 00:18:13,499
for internal HTTP services, no databases.

249
00:18:13,986 --> 00:18:17,260
Jules has no private
network option at all.

250
00:18:17,796 --> 00:18:22,703
Kiro needs your Git server and your
services reachable from the internet.

251
00:18:23,446 --> 00:18:27,775
And, Devin LangGraph, Cloud,
and Vercel get into your

252
00:18:27,775 --> 00:18:29,748
network only on enterprise plan.

253
00:18:29,984 --> 00:18:33,540
Cloudflare's private network is
still in beta, and Claude Code

254
00:18:33,548 --> 00:18:35,700
has to run in a separate sandbox.

255
00:18:35,973 --> 00:18:40,974
So none of them could reach my
databases without an enterprise deal

256
00:18:41,010 --> 00:18:46,214
or a beta, and none of them gives
me durable phases, loops, and gates.

257
00:18:46,688 --> 00:18:53,279
That part was still mine to build So
here is the whole path in one line.

258
00:18:53,590 --> 00:18:56,419
Try fast, fail fast, change fast.

259
00:18:56,445 --> 00:19:03,047
I tried the simplest thing, a prompt on a
runner, and it failed fast at phase eight.

260
00:19:03,075 --> 00:19:08,113
So I moved the plan out of the
prompt and into a workflow engine.

261
00:19:08,235 --> 00:19:13,438
I picked Temporal, partly because I
knew it well from past work, because

262
00:19:13,449 --> 00:19:17,608
in one of my previous companies,
I built a few things with it, so

263
00:19:17,642 --> 00:19:19,730
I could move fast from day one.

264
00:19:20,813 --> 00:19:24,574
And it also, of course, it
met everything on my list.

265
00:19:24,599 --> 00:19:31,792
It runs on my VPC, it handles hours-long
steps, loops, and human gates.

266
00:19:31,830 --> 00:19:38,166
It never re-rewinds a finished
phase, and it lets me read every run.

267
00:19:38,554 --> 00:19:42,577
the price is that I run the
database, tune the heartbeats,

268
00:19:42,605 --> 00:19:44,575
and wrap the Claude SDK myself.

269
00:19:45,005 --> 00:19:47,855
that's my choice for my kind of pipeline.

270
00:19:47,934 --> 00:19:51,893
It's not a ranking that I would
say is general for everyone.

271
00:19:52,596 --> 00:19:56,528
You heard my considerations, and you
need to make your own considerations

272
00:19:56,563 --> 00:19:58,083
when you are going to choose.

273
00:19:59,300 --> 00:20:03,608
If you've never used Temporal,
here it is in one slide.

274
00:20:03,686 --> 00:20:09,625
You write a workflow, which is plain
code, like a for loop over the phases.

275
00:20:10,116 --> 00:20:17,070
It decides what runs next, and it has
to decide the same way every time.

276
00:20:17,254 --> 00:20:22,535
The real work, like calling the model,
saving files, and running checks

277
00:20:22,552 --> 00:20:29,040
happens in activities which can fail and
retry on ev-- on, on their own, okay?

278
00:20:29,315 --> 00:20:34,633
Both run in a worker, which is
just your own process, and the

279
00:20:34,633 --> 00:20:40,493
server writes every step and every
result into an event history.

280
00:20:40,882 --> 00:20:46,275
So if a worker dies, another one
reads the history and carries on.

281
00:20:47,377 --> 00:20:51,087
So what happens when a worker die?

282
00:20:51,989 --> 00:21:01,377
worker A finished P zero and P one,
and the results are in the history.

283
00:21:02,301 --> 00:21:06,543
In the middle of P two,
a deploy kills the pod.

284
00:21:07,018 --> 00:21:12,596
Nobody notices right away because Temporal
waits for a heartbeat or a timeout.

285
00:21:13,360 --> 00:21:21,931
Then worker B picks up, it replace the
workflow, and P zero and P one come

286
00:21:21,934 --> 00:21:24,211
straight from the history for free.

287
00:21:24,524 --> 00:21:29,345
But P two was still running
when the pod died, so it runs

288
00:21:29,347 --> 00:21:31,947
again and you pay for it twice.

289
00:21:32,485 --> 00:21:38,523
So the step that was running when the
pod died is where the money leaks.

290
00:21:39,090 --> 00:21:40,282
Keep that in mind.

291
00:21:40,385 --> 00:21:42,165
I will come back to it in a few minutes.

292
00:21:43,220 --> 00:21:49,311
Now that you know how Temporal works,
here is my real pipeline on top of it.

293
00:21:50,076 --> 00:21:56,816
It has 11 phases and the whole thing
in one plane for loop over them.

294
00:21:57,381 --> 00:22:04,218
Each phase has one of four shapes,
and you can see them at the bottom.

295
00:22:04,909 --> 00:22:08,104
A single phase is one agent doing a job.

296
00:22:08,735 --> 00:22:14,510
A gated phase is an agent and
a critic who checks the work.

297
00:22:14,783 --> 00:22:19,161
A fan-out runs many agents
side by side, and a loop runs

298
00:22:19,260 --> 00:22:21,719
checks, fixes, and tries again.

299
00:22:23,011 --> 00:22:29,153
The first four phases, intake and
investigate, are single agents.

300
00:22:30,164 --> 00:22:37,447
Planning is gated, so an Opus critic reads
the plan, it can send it back once, and

301
00:22:37,469 --> 00:22:41,600
if it rejects it, the run goes to a human.

302
00:22:42,462 --> 00:22:46,895
Then comes the build itself,
which can take up to one hour.

303
00:22:47,572 --> 00:22:55,259
Next comes testing A unit test and
end-to-end test run as a fan-out.

304
00:22:56,120 --> 00:23:03,034
Then QA checks the results against the
spec and a live UI, and anything that

305
00:23:03,039 --> 00:23:07,080
fails goes straight to the fixed step.

306
00:23:08,049 --> 00:23:11,135
Then the review done by
three different models.

307
00:23:11,339 --> 00:23:15,926
Three different reviewers catch more
than three copies of the same one.

308
00:23:16,722 --> 00:23:21,975
Then a loop fixes what the
review found for up to two runs.

309
00:23:22,267 --> 00:23:24,733
Translations fan out for twelve languages.

310
00:23:25,683 --> 00:23:28,897
That's simple work, so it runs on Haiku.

311
00:23:29,473 --> 00:23:32,391
Opus only goes where judgment matters.

312
00:23:32,654 --> 00:23:37,501
Then one more gating step opens the
pull request with a critic checking it.

313
00:23:38,331 --> 00:23:41,739
And the last phase is the CI guardrail.

314
00:23:42,692 --> 00:23:47,979
It loops until CI is green,
up to five tries, and only a

315
00:23:48,036 --> 00:23:50,151
pass marks the run as done.

316
00:23:50,769 --> 00:23:54,048
Which brings me to the
line at the bottom right.

317
00:23:54,450 --> 00:24:00,657
Every run starts as escalated
and has to earn its done badge.

318
00:24:00,759 --> 00:24:01,185
Okay?

319
00:24:01,781 --> 00:24:03,985
And this is everything around it.

320
00:24:04,518 --> 00:24:09,181
A trigger from GitHub, which
is now all the runner does.

321
00:24:09,893 --> 00:24:16,135
An API in Temporal, two workers,
storage, tracing, and a model.

322
00:24:16,418 --> 00:24:20,777
Every box here is something I
have to deploy and keep running.

323
00:24:21,342 --> 00:24:27,478
And one small detail, the two
workers have two separate disks.

324
00:24:27,657 --> 00:24:28,553
Remember that.

325
00:24:28,619 --> 00:24:32,741
I will come back to it in my, one of
the favorite weeks bug that I had.

326
00:24:33,321 --> 00:24:35,624
now, we back to the money.

327
00:24:35,682 --> 00:24:37,441
Remember the reply slide.

328
00:24:37,930 --> 00:24:43,100
When a pod dies in the middle
of a model call, that call runs

329
00:24:43,102 --> 00:24:45,249
again, and you pay for it twice.

330
00:24:45,271 --> 00:24:52,793
Here is how I write each phase to
keep that leak as small as I can.

331
00:24:53,497 --> 00:24:56,472
I call this shape the thin controller.

332
00:24:56,841 --> 00:25:02,427
The controller is a small piece of
code that only picks the next phase.

333
00:25:02,626 --> 00:25:06,349
One step calls the model,
which is where the money goes.

334
00:25:06,595 --> 00:25:11,381
Another step save the results,
and the third one, the real check.

335
00:25:11,576 --> 00:25:16,643
If the worker dies after the model
call, the retry finds the saved

336
00:25:16,667 --> 00:25:18,697
result and doesn't pay again.

337
00:25:19,243 --> 00:25:25,065
In my code, there is one
function, one phase, and it's

338
00:25:25,084 --> 00:25:27,120
the only place that calls Claude.

339
00:25:27,779 --> 00:25:30,317
At least that was the theory.

340
00:25:30,524 --> 00:25:30,899
Okay?

341
00:25:31,311 --> 00:25:34,441
that was the theory, and I wanted a proof.

342
00:25:34,824 --> 00:25:36,678
Two questions bothered me.

343
00:25:37,499 --> 00:25:39,865
Does the engine protect my money?

344
00:25:41,014 --> 00:25:45,257
By itself or does the
shape of my code does it?

345
00:25:45,932 --> 00:25:51,606
And is Temporal spatial here or
would any engine do the same?

346
00:25:51,868 --> 00:25:58,792
So I built one tiny job and crashed it
on purpose on six different engines.

347
00:25:59,335 --> 00:26:01,307
I didn't use a real model for this.

348
00:26:01,454 --> 00:26:05,937
I used a fake one, a small
stand-in with no AI inside.

349
00:26:06,355 --> 00:26:14,576
It just waits a few seconds and charge a
fixed price like a real model call would.

350
00:26:15,051 --> 00:26:19,444
and it writes every charge to
a bill outside the engine, so

351
00:26:19,494 --> 00:26:22,105
the engine can't hide a charge.

352
00:26:22,498 --> 00:26:23,373
Here is the job.

353
00:26:24,194 --> 00:26:31,698
A plan cost fifty cents, edit cost
a dollar fifty, and writes a file.

354
00:26:32,330 --> 00:26:35,267
verify rereads the file for free.

355
00:26:35,471 --> 00:26:42,601
So a clean run cost exactly two
dollar and anything above $2

356
00:26:42,640 --> 00:26:45,308
means I paid for something twice.

357
00:26:45,914 --> 00:26:47,087
this is the bill.

358
00:26:47,629 --> 00:26:52,142
The fake model writes a charge
line when a call starts and

359
00:26:52,336 --> 00:26:54,153
a done line when it finishes.

360
00:26:54,799 --> 00:26:59,375
The engine never sees this
file, so it can't fool me.

361
00:27:00,135 --> 00:27:01,393
Then I click the job.

362
00:27:01,495 --> 00:27:02,095
I, I…

363
00:27:02,117 --> 00:27:04,468
Sorry, I kill the job, three ways.

364
00:27:04,998 --> 00:27:10,356
A hard kill in the middle of the
edit call, a normal shutdown,

365
00:27:10,368 --> 00:27:15,290
like a deploy, and a hard kill
right after the file is written,

366
00:27:15,520 --> 00:27:17,594
before the engine could record it.

367
00:27:18,145 --> 00:27:19,699
And this is the real test.

368
00:27:19,993 --> 00:27:22,367
The same job written two ways.

369
00:27:22,773 --> 00:27:28,797
Variant A is the natural way
most of us would write it.

370
00:27:29,038 --> 00:27:34,179
One big step or a model call right
inside the orchestration code.

371
00:27:34,363 --> 00:27:38,449
And variant B is the thin
controller from the last slide.

372
00:27:39,202 --> 00:27:46,875
Every model call and every file write
is its own small step, and the step is

373
00:27:46,880 --> 00:27:50,160
skipped if its file is already saved.

374
00:27:50,777 --> 00:27:56,366
And I run both version on six
engines, Temporal, Restate, Inngest,

375
00:27:56,387 --> 00:27:58,654
LangGraph, DBOS, and Prefect.

376
00:27:59,527 --> 00:28:04,881
If the engine alone protects my money,
both versions should cost about the same.

377
00:28:05,141 --> 00:28:06,815
So let's see what actually happened.

378
00:28:07,107 --> 00:28:09,746
So here's what the bill looked like.

379
00:28:10,704 --> 00:28:12,772
Each row is one engine.

380
00:28:13,493 --> 00:28:22,437
the line at $2 is a clean run, so anything
to the right of it was paid twice.

381
00:28:22,721 --> 00:28:28,713
First, the natural way with the model
call and the file written in one big step.

382
00:28:29,083 --> 00:28:34,049
Restate, DBOS, and Prefect
cost $4, double the clean run.

383
00:28:34,343 --> 00:28:42,133
LangGraph cost six, Inngest cost fourteen,
and even twelve with no crash at all,

384
00:28:42,279 --> 00:28:44,920
just because of its default settings.

385
00:28:45,218 --> 00:28:50,563
And Temporal, the engine I picked,
is one run, its one run cost

386
00:28:50,677 --> 00:28:53,731
$10, five times the clean run.

387
00:28:54,217 --> 00:28:58,290
So picking a good engine
did not protect my money.

388
00:28:58,786 --> 00:29:05,819
Now, the same job with the thin controller
When I killed it in the middle of a model

389
00:29:05,862 --> 00:29:09,368
call, every engine landed at three fifty.

390
00:29:09,688 --> 00:29:14,483
That's the one call that was
running, paid again and nothing more.

391
00:29:15,206 --> 00:29:19,493
And when the crash came after
the file was saved, every engine

392
00:29:19,501 --> 00:29:22,321
landed at exactly two dollars.

393
00:29:23,058 --> 00:29:24,523
Nothing was paid twice.

394
00:29:24,567 --> 00:29:31,038
So the engine did not save my
money, the shape of my code did.

395
00:29:31,281 --> 00:29:37,044
Every engine paid twice when the model
call and the file write shared one step.

396
00:29:38,029 --> 00:29:41,007
And this is the part
that worried me the most.

397
00:29:41,731 --> 00:29:47,347
In all those runs where I paid
twice, did the engine warn me?

398
00:29:48,334 --> 00:29:53,719
Here is what each engine showed
next to what the bill said.

399
00:29:54,020 --> 00:29:56,361
Temporal showed one clean activity.

400
00:29:57,074 --> 00:30:02,455
The retry only showed up if
you dig into the raw JSON.

401
00:30:02,577 --> 00:30:05,103
The bill said ten dollars or four.

402
00:30:05,607 --> 00:30:12,656
Restate said completed with success and
even reported its own cost as two dollars.

403
00:30:13,112 --> 00:30:15,059
The bill said three fifty.

404
00:30:15,965 --> 00:30:20,819
Inngest said completed each step once.

405
00:30:20,899 --> 00:30:25,436
The bill said six dollars, and
that was without any crash.

406
00:30:25,596 --> 00:30:29,371
LangGraph showed no error
in thirty-four runs.

407
00:30:29,984 --> 00:30:36,170
The bill said six dollars when
two workers resumed the same run.

408
00:30:36,847 --> 00:30:43,391
DBOS showed an empty list of steps
and also reported two dollars.

409
00:30:43,474 --> 00:30:45,262
The bill said three fifty.

410
00:30:45,651 --> 00:30:47,827
Prefect marked every task completed.

411
00:30:48,322 --> 00:30:50,120
The bill said four.

412
00:30:50,234 --> 00:30:58,259
So every engine showed success, and not
one of them showed me the second charge.

413
00:30:58,865 --> 00:31:03,662
That's why you count your money
outside the engine and don't

414
00:31:03,696 --> 00:31:05,749
pick an engine by its own price.

415
00:31:05,811 --> 00:31:11,115
The engine costs cents per run, but
the model costs dollars per run.

416
00:31:11,401 --> 00:31:13,786
So this is what you need
to pay attention for.

417
00:31:14,625 --> 00:31:18,737
Now to part four, what actually broke?

418
00:31:19,819 --> 00:31:25,577
These are my war stories
and what each one taught me.

419
00:31:26,015 --> 00:31:31,005
Some of them will find you no
matter which tool you pick, okay?

420
00:31:31,850 --> 00:31:37,243
I will start with the one that hurt
the most, and it came straight from

421
00:31:37,260 --> 00:31:39,025
the money problem I showed you.

422
00:31:40,357 --> 00:31:47,367
To avoid paying twice, I added a simple
rule before a phase calls Claude.

423
00:31:48,186 --> 00:31:51,708
It checks if its output
file is already there.

424
00:31:52,518 --> 00:31:55,129
If it's not, it calls the model.

425
00:31:56,569 --> 00:32:01,014
If it is there, it skips the
call, pays nothing, and the

426
00:32:01,017 --> 00:32:02,706
next phase reads the file.

427
00:32:03,055 --> 00:32:08,391
But one day, that file was full of error
text from an earlier attempt that failed.

428
00:32:08,795 --> 00:32:13,901
So the code skipped the model,
paid zero dollars, and happily

429
00:32:13,909 --> 00:32:15,853
shipped an empty pull request.

430
00:32:16,978 --> 00:32:20,135
Now, the check is stricter.

431
00:32:20,327 --> 00:32:25,364
Old files get cleaned from the pod
before the run, and the file only

432
00:32:25,366 --> 00:32:32,476
counts if it parses, has its verdict,
and was made from the same inputs.

433
00:32:32,799 --> 00:32:38,508
And I still need that check, because
a crash right after a successful

434
00:32:38,554 --> 00:32:40,590
call is exactly when you pay twice.

435
00:32:40,967 --> 00:32:45,615
The provider already billed you,
and the retry would pay again.

436
00:32:46,638 --> 00:32:48,376
And this is my favorite bug.

437
00:32:48,685 --> 00:32:54,312
Translations went missing, and it
was different languages on every run.

438
00:32:54,521 --> 00:33:01,217
I was sure the agents were
to blame, but they were not.

439
00:33:01,455 --> 00:33:05,503
Remember the two workers,
each with its own disk that I

440
00:33:05,503 --> 00:33:06,798
showed you a few slides ago?

441
00:33:07,147 --> 00:33:14,242
So the twelve translations tasks go into
one queue, and the scheduler decides

442
00:33:14,318 --> 00:33:16,937
which worker picks up which language.

443
00:33:17,340 --> 00:33:18,482
You don't control it.

444
00:33:18,723 --> 00:33:25,725
one step wrote its input file on worker
A when the next step landed on worker B.

445
00:33:26,275 --> 00:33:28,291
the file just was not there.

446
00:33:28,536 --> 00:33:34,378
So the agents on B wrote polite
essays about missing file, and those

447
00:33:34,405 --> 00:33:41,589
essays become the output itself Then
the merge ran in worker A, and it's

448
00:33:41,676 --> 00:33:43,773
only saw the file written there.

449
00:33:43,979 --> 00:33:47,823
Seven of the 12 languages were missing.

450
00:33:48,153 --> 00:33:52,773
So the randomness came from the
scheduler, not from the model.

451
00:33:53,483 --> 00:33:56,938
Now, nothing goes to the local disk.

452
00:33:57,109 --> 00:34:04,464
Every file goes to S3, and the worker
only passes its addresses and a hash.

453
00:34:04,679 --> 00:34:10,132
Since then, all hundred and eighty-four
keys come through every time.

454
00:34:10,961 --> 00:34:14,434
So how did I find all of this?

455
00:34:15,186 --> 00:34:18,119
I traced every model call with Langfuse.

456
00:34:19,043 --> 00:34:22,761
That's an open source tool
that shows what the model saw,

457
00:34:23,349 --> 00:34:25,373
what it did, and what it cost.

458
00:34:25,832 --> 00:34:30,454
With that, most bugs took
minutes to find and not days.

459
00:34:30,980 --> 00:34:32,641
Here are eight of them.

460
